  "question": {
    "text": "If [ ] denotes the greatest integer function, then the integral $\\int_0^\\pi[\\cos x d x$ is equal to:",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "DxWRv",
      "text": "\n$\\frac{\\pi}{2}$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "nbbiR",
      "text": "\n0\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "lwbUw",
      "text": "\n$-1$\n",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "XRgFJ",
      "text": "\n$-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "Let $\\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^\\pi[\\cos x] d x$\n$$\n\\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^\\pi[\\cos (\\pi-x)] d x=\\int_0^\\pi[-\\cos x] d x\n$$\nOn adding (1) and (2), we get\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n&2 \\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^\\pi[\\cos x] d x+\\int_0^\\pi[-\\cos x] d x \\\\\n&2 \\mathrm{I}=\\int_0^\\pi[\\cos x]+[-\\cos x] d x \\\\\n&2 \\mathrm{I} \\\\\n&=\\int_0^\\pi-1 d x \\quad(\\because[x]+[-x]=-1 \\text { if } x \\notin Z) \\\\\n&2 \\mathrm{I}=-\\left.x\\right|_0 ^\\pi=-\\pi \\\\\n&\\Rightarrow \\mathrm{I}=\\frac{-\\pi}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
    "image": null
  },
